\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Elimination and retained observables}
\date{2026-10-20}

\p{Eliminating selected real field values produces both a
[new quadratic action and a normalization factor](fdip-000D).
The prerequisites are a [positive quadratic action](fdip-0004),
its [normalized Gaussian law](fdip-0005), and the
[reference measure and orthogonal product volume](fdip-003U).
Inverse precision first determines covariance; elimination then
identifies the retained law.}

\p{The retained expectation agrees with the original expectation on
[observables pulled back from the retained variables](fdip-000E).
Opposite vertices of the [four-cycle](fdip-000F) make the normalization
and covariance explicit. The [conditional kernel](fdip-003A)
distinguishes fixing variables from averaging them, while
[singular eliminated directions](fdip-0039) explain when the
integration space itself must change.}

\p{For a [formal quartic perturbation](fdip-0046), successive
Gaussian eliminations agree with direct elimination through second
order in the coupling.
The [three-coordinate example](fdip-0047) displays the generated
terms and a retained connected four-point function;
[stationary elimination](fdip-0048) distinguishes the tree
contribution from fluctuation terms.}

\transclude{fdip-000B}
